  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 125 AM EDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nUpper-level ridging over the eastern CONUS will gradually push \neastward into the Atlantic as it becomes flattened by an upper-level \ntrough during the second half of the week. At the surface, high \npressure situated over the western Atlantic is expected to maintain \na general easterly flow across South Florida through the weekend. \nUnder this regime, isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms \nmigrating onshore from the Atlantic waters will be possible along \nthe East Coast each morning. As daytime heating ramps up and sea \nbreezes push inland, the primary focus for convection will shift \ntoward the interior and SW Florida, where activity will be maximized \nduring the afternoon and early evening hours each day. \n\nCurrent LREF ensemble mean guidance indicates that atmospheric \nmoisture content will remain slightly above normal through the \nperiod, with PWATs generally in the 1.9-2.1 inch range. However, \nglobal models continue to show a Saharan dust plume approaching the \nregion this weekend into early next week. While the highest dust \nconcentrations should remain well to the south of the peninsula, any \nmid-level dry air intrusion could potentially enhance mid-level \nlapse rates and DCAPE, thereby increasing the potential for \nlocalized severe winds in robust afternoon convection. Confidence in \nthis scenario remains low at this time, given uncertainty regarding \nthe timing and extent of the dry air intrusion. Otherwise, the \npotential for severe weather is expected to remain fairly limited, \nwith wind shear staying weak and 500 mb temperatures forecast to \nremain near to slightly warmer than climatology through the period. \n\nHigh temperatures will rise into the lower 90s across most areas \nthrough early next week, reaching the mid 90s over interior SW \nFlorida. Overnight lows will range from the low 70s across the \ninterior to the low 80s along the coasts. Major HeatRisk \nprobabilities will gradually increase across coastal and metro areas \ninto the weekend as persistent onshore flow keeps heat indices \nelevated and limits overnight cooling.",
